On Sunday, August 31, 2014 10:11:02 PM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> 1.  Rather than this:
>
>   for(j in 1:J) {
>       for(t in 1:T)
>             lambda[t,j] ~ gamma(nu[j]/2,nu[j]/2) ;
>
> you can use:
>
>   for (t in 1:T)
>     lambda[t] ~ gamma(nu / 2, nu / 2);
>
> Even better would be to not divide nu by 2 twice, but to do it once
> and assign it to a temporary.
>
> 2.   Replace
>
>     for (t in 1:T) {
>         for (j in 1:J)
>             lambdainvsqrt[t][j] <- inv_sqrt(lambda[t][j]) ;
>         LSigma[t] <- LRho * diag_matrix(sigma) * diag_matrix(lambdainvsqrt[t]) ;
>     }
>
> with
>
>    {
>       matrix[J,J] LRho_times_diag_matrix_sigma;
>       LRho_sigma <- diag_post_multiply(LRho, sigma);
>       for (t in 1:T) {
>         for (j in 1:J)
>             lambdainvsqrt[t][j] <- inv_sqrt(lambda[t][j]) ;
>         LSigma[t] <- diag_post_multiply(LRho_sigma, lambda_inv_sqrt[t]);
>     }
>
> It may be even faster in a loop due to the sparsity.  In just
> two dimensions, you can just write
>
>   LSigma[t,1,1] <- ...
>   LSigma[t,2,2] <- ...
>   LSigma[t,2,1] <- ...
>   LSigma[t,1,2] <- 0;  // lower triangular
>
> with ... replaced by the reuslt of all the above matrix multiplication.
> Make sure to save intermediate computations.
>
> 3.  If you don't need to save lambda_inv_sqrt[t], make it a local in the
> same scope.
>
>
>
> 4.  This isn't a speed issue per se, but Andrew's been very down on the constrained
> uniform priors (though we still have some in our manual):
>
>     vector<lower=0,upper=100>[J] sigma ;    // scale of multivariate vector
>     vector<lower=2,upper=30>[J] nu ;        // degrees of freedom of multivariate vector
>
> Rather than this, you can just put a real prior on sigma and nu and leave
> them unconstrained (well, they need lower=0 at least).
>
> 5.  Given that you only have a two-dimensional Cholesky factor, you could
> just parameterize directly on the correlation rho.
>
> - Bob
>
>
>
> On Aug 31, 2014, at 8:27 PM, Ravi Sastry <ravis...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> > After making the appropriate changes to my parameter declarations and priors, my model is working. I've tested it on simulated data up to 20 dimensions.
> >
> > Code is attached if you're interested. Any suggestions to help express the model more efficiently would be appreciated.
>
>
>
> >
> > Ravi
> >
> > On Sunday, August 17, 2014 5:46:58 PM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > Yes, the doc says this all over the place.
> >
> > - Bob
> >
> > On Aug 15, 2014, at 11:52 PM, Ravi Sastry <ravis...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > > John and Luc,
> > >
> > > I was (of course) hoping to let Stan generate the full joint log density and gradient on its own. But if my current approach doesn't work out, and since there is no support yet for copula distributions, I will need to explicitly specify the log density as you suggest. Those approximations for erf() may prove essential.
> > >
> > > Herra,
> > >
> > > I'll make the change to my declared bounds to see if it fixes things. I hope so! But my simpler model with univariate t data using the scale mixture representation had the same bounds and priors on the relevant parameters, and that converged without issue. Do you know if the Stan documentation warns against specifying a prior whose support doesn't exactly match the declared bounds?
> >
> >
> > > (As you see from my code, I assumed that having prior support a subset of the declared bounds would be OK.)
> >
> > You can only do it the other way around.
> >
> > For instance, a half-Cauchy prior can be defined by
> >
> >   parameters {
> >     real<lower=0> sigma;
> >     ...
> >   }
> >   model
> >     sigma ~ cauchy(0,2.5);
> >     ...
> >
> > You only need an explicit truncation when either one of the bounds or
> > one of the parameters of the distribution is a parameter --- otherwise, you
> > can just drop it, as above, where it would be T[0,].
> >
> > But if you do this:
> >
> >   parameters {
> >     real sigma;
> >     ...
> >   }
> >   model {
> >     sigma ~ lognormal(0,1);
> >     ...
> >
> > you'll almost certainly run into problems sampling when sigma goes below 0
> > either doing initialization or during the Hamiltonian dynamics.  That causes
> > a rejection, and if it's an init, that's fatal.
> >
