From ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com  Sun Sep  7 09:42:34 2014
From: ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com (Michael Betancourt)
Date: Sun, 07 Sep 2014 09:42:34 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Problem with multimodality (Dirichlet parameters in one chain stuck near 0)
Message-ID: <xEG18UzoCGo.umn1Mp4TQ4EJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <xEG18UzoCGo.DHlzKPnokrUJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <xEG18UzoCGo.EeXeqPox3sAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <xEG18UzoCGo.DHlzKPnokrUJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/xEG18UzoCGo/m/umn1Mp4TQ4EJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This is not multimodality but rather a failure of convergence.  In particular, you should be seeing n_divergent=1
for many, if not most, iterations.  The problem is the extreme curvature near zero which causes the gradients,
and hence the HMC transitions, to fail.

An immediate option is to decrease the step size (or increase the target average acceptance probability in the
adaptation).  Alternatively you can try to find a parameterization that reduces the change in curvature.  Lastly
you can reduce the curvature by smoothing out your model, as you have done.

