To follow up again: I tried setting adapt_delta to .9, .95, and .99, and it didn't seem to affect the output. One or two chains would always get stuck somewhere far from the others. Also, the problem doesn't seem to have much to do with n_divergent. In every chain, n_divergent=1 in 2–5% of iterations, and the proportion of 1s appeared to be unrelated to getting stuck. Specifying a more informative prior is more effective at avoiding the problem but isn't fool-proof. Also, I should clarify that I was already declaring props as a simplex, and that I am using rstan Version 2.4.0.

Thanks!
Devin

On Sunday, September 7, 2014 10:27:56 AM UTC-4, Michael Betancourt wrote:
> Just declare props as a simplex type, which will implicitly apply the best transforms.
>
> In CmdStan just set adapt delta=0.9 (or 0.95, 0.99, increasing until n_divergence is always zero).  The other defaults will change (or not) as necessary automatically.  In general increasing the target average acceptance probability may lead to smaller efficiency but it will always lead to a more robust sampler so it’s a very safe knob to turn.
>
> On Sep 7, 2014, at 2:09 PM, Devin Caughey <cau...@mit.edu> wrote:
>
> > Thanks very much for the explanation, Michael. In terms of reparameterization, I have played around with applying a logit transformation to the proportions, along the lines of:
> > for (t in 1:T) {
> >   for (k in 1:N) {
> >     logit_props[t, k] <- logit(props[t, k]);
> >     increment_log_prob(-(log(props[t, k]) + log1m(props[t, k])));
> >   }
> >   if (t > 1) {
> >     logit_props[t] ~ normal(logit_props[t - 1], innovation_sd);
> >   }
> > }
> >
> >
> > Would this reparameterization reduce the curvature as you suggested?
> >
> > I will also look into manually increasing the step size, though I am a little wary about overriding the automated defaults.
> >
> > Thanks!
> > Devin
> >
> > On Sunday, September 7, 2014 5:42:34 AM UTC-4, Michael Betancourt wrote:
> > > This is not multimodality but rather a failure of convergence.  In particular, you should be seeing n_divergent=1
> > > for many, if not most, iterations.  The problem is the extreme curvature near zero which causes the gradients,
> > > and hence the HMC transitions, to fail.
> > >
> > > An immediate option is to decrease the step size (or increase the target average acceptance probability in the
> > > adaptation).  Alternatively you can try to find a parameterization that reduces the change in curvature.  Lastly
> > > you can reduce the curvature by smoothing out your model, as you have done.
> > >
> > > On Sep 7, 2014, at 3:20 AM, Devin Caughey <cau...@mit.edu <http://mit.edu/>> wrote:
> > >
> > > > To follow up on my original email, I tried using a somewhat more informative (but still pretty weak) prior and it seems to have solved the multi-modality problem, at least for now.
> > > >
> > > > On Saturday, September 6, 2014 5:58:16 PM UTC-4, Devin Caughey wrote:
> > > > > Hello,
> > > > >
> > > > > I am estimating a version of a dynamic ecological inference model. The model allows future information on interior cell proportions to inform estimates for other periods when only the marginal proportions are observed. It does so with a Dirichlet transition model for the cell proportions, as in:
> > > > > props[t] ~ dirichlet(props[t - 1] * priorN);
> > > > > I use a uniform prior for the proportions in t=1.
> > > > >
> > > > > Stan usually handles the model fine, but when I estimate it with 10 chains, 9 of the chains will converge on a single mode but one chain often gets stuck in a different part of the parameter space. The posterior means looks something like this:
> > > > >           parameters
