Although this is an ugly way several folks on this list and outside the list suggested that mixture of Gaussian Processes (GP) might be a solution.  Basically this is calibration of computer simulation data with experimental data.  Green dots indicate experimental data, blue line indicate computer simulation.  Red line is what I have arrived to using Gibbs kernel.  Since of the sudden increase of response (Cp) at input (arclength) close to 0.5 the experimental and computer simulation data don't match each another.  Physically it makes complete sense since those correspond to different flows.  Thus the idea is to represent each flow as two separate GPs with separate set of hyper-parameters.   By assigning different priors on hyper-parameters for each GP one can manipulate smoothness etc.  For example in the chart below GP on the right side is not smooth enough.  Sigmoid was suggested as a way to ensure that those GPs don't interact two much when considered as a mixture.  For this example I am thinking that I could use one GP on the right and normal distribution on the left which I assume will remove identifiability problem.

